  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSurface high pressure centered eastern NY/eastern PA will\ncontinue to maintain dry weather across the region today and\ntonight. Mid and high level clouds will continue to stream\nacross the area in warm and moist advection aloft ahead of a\nshortwave across the Midwest. This shortwave trough is expected\nto weaken as it moves east, and crosses the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion on Monday. At the surface, a weak warm front is also\nprogged to lift NE on Monday. Moisture is expected to be limited\nwith this system, though a few sprinkles or brief light rain is\npossible, mainly across OH, as the wave and surface front cross.\nOnly a brief mention of slight chance POPs were included mainly\nwest of I-77 on Monday. \n\nA ridge is then expected to build across the eastern CONUS\nTuesday into Wednesday, ahead of a deepening central CONUS\ntrough. This ridge should result in dry weather and the warmest\ntemperatures of the week, with rising 500 mb heights. While it\nis not likely to set any record highs, temperatures are expected\nto be 15 to 20 degrees above average.\n\nThe central CONUS trough, and associated surface front, are \nexpected to track eastward and approach the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion late Wednesday. Rain chances gradually increase late in\nthe day, peaking Wednesday night into Thursday as the trough and\nsurface front cross the region. Model ensembles then indicate\nbroad upper troughing will persist across the NE CONUS into\nSaturday, with periodic rain chances continuing as individual\nshortwaves rotate through the main trough.",
